from lib17 import *
# ===== Ethics (Mock 17, from Mock 8 Q39, Mock 6 Q43, Mock 10 Q9): finder's fee disclosure, fair dealing vs communication vs priority of transactions, paid travel =====
body="""Part 1
Niamh Gallagher, CFA, who is based in Dublin, runs the asset management firm that manages the assets for the Bright Futures Social Impact Private Equity Fund (the Fund), which is registered in Ireland. The general partner of the Fund has asked Gallagher to meet with Greta Sundberg, CFA, a pension asset consultant, to find out whether Sundberg will pitch the Fund to her institutional clients looking for an environmental, social, and governance (ESG) approach.

During an introductory meeting that lasts about an hour, Sundberg asks Gallagher to give an overview of the Fund. Gallagher makes the following statement:

"Let me explain how we select investments. I'll give you the prospectus, too, for your further reference, because it contains all the details, and they're quite extensive. We identify potential investments from around the globe through our own analysts' research within the education and health care industries. We also use third-party agents to find potential investments. If we make an investment in the companies they present to us, we pay them a finder's fee. One such agent we use is Impact Alliance Consultants, an asset consulting firm that has adopted the CFA Institute Code and Standards."

Part 2
Jonas Pettersen, CFA, is a portfolio manager at Corbett Investment Management, an asset management and research focused organization.

Pettersen joins the board of Chatterly.com, a smaller social media competitor to Skyrelay. Based on his knowledge of Skyrelay, Pettersen believes the stock of Chatterly is a good investment, even though it is not yet profitable. Pettersen purchases shares of Chatterly's IPO for all the clients he currently manages.

The Chatterly investment is profitable on the first day of trading, doubling from its opening price. Corbett's research report, due out the next day, will recommend investors hold their Chatterly shares, because the multifactor valuation model used by Corbett shows the stock is still undervalued. However, Pettersen tells all his clients simultaneously they should sell their shares because he believes Chatterly is overvalued and the stock price will fall soon. Pettersen notes he has followed through on this belief by selling his personal holdings of Chatterly shares. Over the next week, the stock declines 75%.

Part 3
Rodrick Valdes, CFA, is a senior research analyst at TTN Capital, a large full-service brokerage and investment firm. Valdes is invited to an analyst conference hosted by Orion Data Centers, a company covered by Valdes. Orion arranges to pay for the commercial flights for all analysts attending the conference. Orion also provides a company-branded notebook as a gift to all analysts who attend the conference. Valdes accepts the travel arrangement and the gift."""
add('M17','Ethical and Professional Standards',body,[
 q("Given Gallagher's statement to Sundberg, which further action should Gallagher least likely take to comply with required or recommended procedures for the CFA Institute Standards of Professional Conduct?",["Notify clients of the finder's fee policy on an annual basis.","Monitor the consultants' compliance policy execution.","Disclose the finder's fee arrangement in the fund prospectus."],0,
  ["Correct. Recommended procedures for Standard VI(C) Referral Fees are to notify clients at least quarterly, not annually.","Incorrect. Impact Alliance claims to follow the Code and Standards; under Standard I(A) Gallagher must not knowingly assist others in violations, so she should seek assurance that they also disclose referral fees.","Incorrect. The use of finder's fees should be disclosed in the prospectus to comply with Standard VI(C)."],
  ["we pay them a finder's fee"],"VI(C) Referral Fees: disclose finder's fee arrangements to clients and prospects (prospectus) and notify clients at least QUARTERLY.",fixed=False),
 q("In relation to Pettersen's handling of the Chatterly stock recommendation, which of the following CFA Institute Standards of Professional Conduct did he least likely violate?",["Communication with Clients","Fair Dealing","Priority of Transactions"],1,
  ["Incorrect. Under Standard V(B) he should have distinguished fact from opinion and disclosed the basis of his analysis, including that it contradicts the firm's research recommendation.","Correct. Under Standard III(B) he treated all clients fairly: his sell recommendation was given to all of his clients simultaneously.","Incorrect. He violated Standard VI(B) Priority of Transactions: clients hold the same stock and should have priority to trade; he sold his personal holdings after telling clients to sell, instead of letting client transactions come first."],
  ["tells all his clients simultaneously they should sell their shares","least likely violate"],"'Least likely violated' = the Standard with NO problem: equal and simultaneous dissemination → fair dealing OK; opinion vs fact, contradicting research and trading ahead of clients → problems.",fixed=False),
 q("Does Valdes violate the Standards regarding the Orion conference?",["Yes, by accepting the travel arrangement and the gift","No","Yes, by accepting the travel arrangement only"],2,
  ["Incorrect. A company-branded notebook is a token item of nominal value; accepting it does not compromise independence.","Incorrect. Accepting paid travel does violate the Standard.","Correct. Under Standard I(B) Independence and Objectivity, best practice is to use commercial transportation at one's own or the firm's expense rather than accept paid travel; a modest branded gift is acceptable."],
  ["Orion arranges to pay for the commercial flights for all analysts attending the conference","company-branded notebook as a gift"],"I(B): do not accept paid travel from the covered company (pay for flights yourself or via your firm); small token gifts are acceptable.",fixed=False),
])
